var e=[{id:`fundamental-frontend-system-design`,pathId:`the-modern-stack`,order:0,title:`Frontend System Design`,subtitle:`"Frontend system design" used to be an oxymoron. In 2026, it's the discipline that decides Web Vitals scores, mobile abandonment rate, and how many engineers can ship in parallel o`,minutes:12,difficulty:4.5,tags:[`CSR`,`Core Web Vitals`,`SSR`,`hydration`],story:{headline:`Six seconds of white screen on the biggest shopping day of the year`,paragraphs:[`Launch day, 6:04 AM. The team had spent a quarter rebuilding checkout as a slick React SPA, and the demo got applause — buttery transitions, instant-feeling, on a 16-inch MacBook Pro. Then the on-call dashboard lit up: mobile conversion down 23%, bounce rate on /checkout up 40%, and session replays showing real users staring at a blank white rectangle for seconds at a time.`,`An engineer grabbed a three-year-old mid-range Android, throttled it to slow 4G with a cold cache, and watched the crime happen in slow motion: 300 ms of near-empty HTML, then 1.4 MB of gzipped JavaScript, then parse, compile, execute — and only then a data fetch, and only then paint. 6.2 seconds of nothing. The SPA hadn't been designed; it had happened. One bundle, client-render everything, hydrate the world — every architectural decision made by accident.`,`The fix wasn't a heroic rewrite. It was system design: server-render the first paint, split the bundle by route, hydrate only the interactive islands, inline the critical CSS, and put a 200 KB budget in CI that fails the build. Two weeks later, p75 mobile LCP was 1.9 seconds and conversion recovered. The lesson stuck: the frontend has system design too — and it decides who waits.`],problem:`Every rendering decision assigns a cost — in seconds, bytes, or dollars — to a specific user. Frontend system design is the discipline of choosing, deliberately, who pays.`},tech:{stack:[`SSR`,`CSR`,`RSC`,`Hydration`,`Islands`,`Route Code Splitting`,`Core Web Vitals`,`Next.js`],note:`The rendering modes, hydration strategies, and budget tooling the team reached for while turning an accidental architecture into a deliberate one.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the MacBook lied to us`,body:[`Let me start with the uncomfortable part: the demo that got applause and the phone in your junk drawer are running two different products. On a 16-inch MacBook with a warm cache and office Wi-Fi, a 1.4 MB client-rendered bundle feels instant. On a three-year-old Android over throttled 4G, that same bundle is a serial waterfall: empty HTML, download, parse, compile, execute, fetch data, then — finally — paint. Six steps, each one waiting for the last.`,`So the first instinct is 'minify harder, tree-shake more, brotli it.' And sure, you'll shave 15%. But you can't shave your way out of a shape problem — as long as the first paint waits on the last step of a client-side waterfall, you're one slow CPU away from a white screen. The fix isn't smaller JavaScript; it's moving the paint earlier in time, which means moving it to a different machine.`],quote:`Your MacBook is a liar. The phone in your junk drawer is the truth.`,table:{headers:[`CSR first-paint step`,`What happens`,`Typical cost on mid-tier mobile`],rows:[[`HTML shell`,`Near-empty div; everything waits on JS`,`~0.3s`],[`JS download`,`1.4 MB gzipped over throttled 4G`,`3-5s`],[`Parse + compile`,`CPU-bound on a slow chipset`,`1-2s`],[`Execute + fetch data`,`Mount React root, then call the API`,`0.5-1s`],[`First paint`,`User sees anything at all`,`6s+`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Frontend System Design`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frontend-system-design.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: where should each pixel render?`,body:[`Once you accept that rendering location is a design decision, the pendulum story makes sense. Server-rendered HTML gave fast first paint but every interaction hit a round trip. SPAs flipped it: glorious interactivity, brutal first paint, JS-blind SEO. The current era is the synthesis — and the frameworks (Next, Remix, Astro, Nuxt, SvelteKit) are really mode orchestrators.`,`The rule I use when someone asks 'SSR or CSR?': stop asking about the app, ask about the route and the component. How dynamic is the data? How interactive is the surface? Marketing page — static, CDN, done. Product page — ISR, static speed with freshness. App shell — SSR and hydrate. One funky interactive widget — island it. Most production apps are a mix, and that's not a compromise; that's the design.`],quote:`The pendulum hasn't stopped swinging. It just got finer-grained.`,table:{headers:[`Mode`,`What it does`,`Strength`,`Weakness`],rows:[[`SSR`,`Server renders HTML per request`,`SEO, fast FCP, works without JS`,`Server cost, cold-cache latency`],[`SSG`,`Static HTML at build time`,`Fastest possible, CDN-served`,`Stale until rebuild`],[`ISR`,`Static, regenerated on demand`,`Static speed plus freshness`,`Complex revalidation`],[`CSR`,`JS bundles render in browser`,`App-like interactivity, simple deploy`,`Slow FCP, JS-blind SEO, mobile cost`],[`RSC`,`Render on server, ship as data`,`Smaller JS, data fetch in components`,`New mental model`],[`Islands`,`Static page, hydrate interactive islands`,`Minimal JS, static-fast`,`Limits app-like interactions`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Frontend System Design`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frontend-system-design.html`}},{heading:`03 · The hydration tax: paying twice for the same UI`,body:[`Now the part that surprises people: even after SSR, you're not done paying. The server sends beautiful HTML, and then the browser downloads the JavaScript and re-renders the exact same tree — just to attach onClick handlers. That's hydration: you pay twice for the same UI, once in HTML and once in JavaScript. On a slow phone, 'I can see the button' and 'the button works' can be seconds apart.`,`So the question becomes: which parts of the page actually need JavaScript? A product description doesn't. A price doesn't. An 'add to cart' does. Islands hydrate only the interactive subtrees. React Server Components go further — server components ship as serialized data and never hydrate at all. Resumability (the Qwik idea) serializes the event handlers so there's no re-execution. Every one of these trades a new mental model for bytes, and on mobile, bytes are seconds.`],quote:`Hydration is paying twice for the same UI. Islands and RSC are how you stop.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`What ships`,`Pros`,`Cons`],rows:[[`Full hydration`,`HTML + full JS tree`,`Simple, one mental model`,`Pays for every component twice`],[`Islands`,`Static HTML + JS per island`,`Tiny JS on content pages`,`Awkward for app-like state`],[`RSC`,`HTML + serialized server components`,`Zero hydration for server parts`,`New mental model, framework coupling`],[`Resumability`,`HTML + serialized handlers`,`Near-zero re-execution`,`Youngest ecosystem`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Frontend System Design`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frontend-system-design.html`}},{heading:`04 · Budgets or it didn't happen`,body:[`Here's where most teams fail — not in choosing SSR versus CSR, but in enforcement. A performance budget that lives in a wiki is a wish. The budget has to fail the build: size-limit or bundlesize in CI, red check on a 201 KB pull request. And it has to be measured where users live — mid-tier Android, throttled 4G, cold cache — not on the machine that signed off the design.`,`And know where the weight actually is. Images dominate page weight; one un-optimized hero can blow your LCP budget single-handedly — so AVIF or WebP, responsive srcset, lazy-load below the fold. Inline the critical above-the-fold CSS and async-load the rest; on most sites that's the single biggest LCP win available. Then watch the real-user numbers — LCP, INP, CLS — as release gates, not dashboard wallpaper.`],quote:`A performance budget that isn't enforced in CI is a wish, not a budget.`,table:{headers:[`Web Vital`,`Good`,`Needs improvement`,`Poor`],rows:[[`LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)`,`under 2.5s`,`2.5-4s`,`over 4s`],[`INP (Interaction to Next Paint)`,`under 200ms`,`200-500ms`,`over 500ms`],[`CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift)`,`under 0.1`,`0.1-0.25`,`over 0.25`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Frontend System Design`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frontend-system-design.html`}},{heading:`05 · Who pays which cost?`,body:[`Zoom out, and frontend system design is really cost accounting. SSG: the build pipeline pays once, the CDN serves nearly free, the user pays nothing. SSR: the server pays compute on every request so the user pays less latency. CSR: the user pays in bytes, battery, and seconds. Islands: the user pays only for what they touch. There is no free option — there's only a choice of who writes the check.`,`The same lens explains micro-frontends, the most expensive word in the vocabulary. They buy team autonomy — ten teams shipping in parallel — and the bill is duplicated dependencies, runtime orchestration, and a module federation tax on every page. Ship a shared UI library across teams and you trade bundle duplication against version drift. None of these choices are wrong; they're just costs. Name the payer before you pick the tool.`],quote:`Frontend system design isn't about rendering. It's about deciding which user pays which cost.`,table:{headers:[`Decision`,`Who pays`,`What they pay`],rows:[[`SSG`,`Build pipeline`,`One-time build, stale-content risk`],[`SSR`,`Server`,`Compute per request`],[`CSR`,`User`,`Bytes, battery, seconds`],[`Islands`,`User`,`Only the island's JS`],[`Micro-frontends`,`Organization`,`Duplicated deps, runtime complexity`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Frontend System Design`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frontend-system-design.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`csr-waterfall`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of the 6-second white screen`,caption:`The client-rendered first paint is a serial waterfall — every step waits on the last, and the user leaves before it finishes.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["User hits /checkout on mid-tier Android"] --> B["Server returns near-empty HTML div"]
    B --> C["Download app.js - 1.4 MB gzipped over slow 4G"]
    C --> D["Parse and compile on a slow CPU"]
    D --> E["Execute bundle, mount React root"]
    E --> F["Fetch checkout data from the API"]
    F --> G["First paint finally at 6.2 seconds"]
    G --> H["User bounced at second 3"]`},{id:`rebuilt-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The rebuilt request path`,caption:`Server-rendered first paint, route-split JS, and islands that hydrate only what moves — paint first, interactivity second.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant U as User - mid-tier Android
    participant C as CDN
    participant S as SSR Server
    U->>C: GET /checkout
    C->>S: cache miss
    S->>S: render tree to HTML server-side
    S-->>U: HTML + inlined critical CSS - FCP at 1.1s
    U->>C: GET checkout-route.js - 96 KB route split
    C-->>U: JS chunk
    U->>U: hydrate interactive islands only - usable at 2.3s
    U->>S: POST /api/pay`}],takeaways:[`Budget initial JS under 200 KB gzipped (aim for 100 KB) and enforce it in CI — a pull request that breaks the budget fails the build.`,`Gate releases on Core Web Vitals measured on mid-tier Android over throttled 4G: LCP under 2.5s, INP under 200ms, CLS under 0.1.`,`Code-split by route: the landing page should never download the dashboard's JavaScript.`,`Attack images first — AVIF/WebP, responsive srcset, lazy-load below the fold; one un-optimized hero can blow the LCP budget single-handedly.`,`Inline above-the-fold critical CSS and async-load the rest — the single biggest LCP win on most sites.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Frontend System Design`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frontend-system-design.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`mj-ka_WCmNdybE`,note:`Design a File Upload Service Like a Senior Engineer covers this from a different angle.`}],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-mobile-architecture`,pathId:`the-modern-stack`,order:1,title:`Mobile Architecture`,subtitle:`Mobile architecture is what happens when the client owns state, the network is unreliable, the OS rations CPU, and the user can install the update next month or never. The intervie`,minutes:11,difficulty:4.5,tags:[`Clean`,`MVVM`,`Native`,`cross-platform`],story:{headline:`412 double charges, one subway tunnel, and a six-day review queue`,paragraphs:[`Launch night for the mobile checkout rewrite. At 11:52 PM, support Slack lights up: riders on the Red Line are getting charged twice. The tunnel eats the API response, OkHttp's retry interceptor fires the POST again, and because nobody put an idempotency key on the request, the backend books a second payment. 412 double charges before anyone even finds the right dashboard.`,`The postmortem digs deeper, and it's uglier than one missing header. The app treated SQLite as a cache and the API as the truth — so every screen was a spinner, airplane mode rendered as an empty screen, and process death in the background wiped half-finished carts. The hotfix itself was ready in nine hours. It shipped six days later, after app-store review. For six days, every launch made it worse.`,`That week rewired how the team thought. A phone is not a thin client with a small screen — it's a distributed-system node that owns state, gets its CPU rationed by the OS, loses the network in tunnels, and can only be patched through a review queue with a multi-day SLA. Everything in this chapter exists because of that week.`],problem:`How do you architect a client that owns state, survives an unreliable network and OS process kills, and ships fixes through a review queue you don't control?`},tech:{stack:[`SQLite / Room`,`SwiftData / GRDB`,`MVVM + UDF`,`Repository Pattern`,`Coroutines / Flow`,`WorkManager / BGTask`,`Idempotency Keys`,`Feature Flags`],note:`The pieces the postmortem rewrite installed: a local DB promoted to source of truth, reactive plumbing from DB to UI, a single repository door over network and storage, OS-scheduled sync, and server-side safety nets for the releases you can't recall.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'the API is the truth' fails on a phone`,body:[`Here's the instinct most web engineers carry over: the server owns the data, the client renders it. On the web that's roughly true — you're one refresh away from consistency. On a phone, your user is in a tunnel, on a plane, in a parking garage with one bar. If the API is the truth, then offline means broken, and 'broken' renders as a spinner.`,`So flip it. The local database — Room on Android, GRDB or SwiftData on iOS — is the single source of truth. The UI reads from the DB. Sync writes to the DB. The network is just the freshener. And here's the part teams skip: make the DB observable. Compose gives you Flow, SwiftUI gives you Combine and Observation, and when a row changes, the UI repaints itself. Nobody hand-notifies a screen at 3 AM.`,`'Why not just cache the API responses?' Because a cache is a snapshot with expiry semantics — it answers 'what did the server say recently?' A database as truth answers 'what do I know?' Those are different questions, and only the second one works offline by construction.`],quote:`On mobile, the server isn't the source of truth — it's the freshener.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`API as truth + spinners`,`Simple mental model, always 'fresh'`,`Offline = broken UI, latency on every screen`,`Kiosk apps on Wi-Fi only`],[`DB as cache with TTL`,`Cheap retrofit onto web habits`,`Stale by design, no offline writes`,`Read-only content apps`],[`DB as source of truth (offline-first)`,`Instant UI, works offline, testable`,`Sync protocol and conflict handling to build`,`Default for real products`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Mobile Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-mobile-architecture.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the repository is the only door`,body:[`Next thought: who's allowed to touch the DB and the network? My rule is nobody — except the repository. The ViewModel asks for 'orders', and the repository decides: serve from local? refresh from network in the background? pull both and merge? The UI never knows, and that ignorance is the feature.`,`Why not let ViewModels call the API directly? Count the ways: every screen invents its own offline story, your business logic gets welded to the HTTP client, and unit testing now requires a simulator. Keep the domain layer framework-free — plain Kotlin or Swift use cases — and it tests in milliseconds without booting an emulator.`,`Mutations get the same treatment, but optimistic. User taps 'like' — write to the local DB immediately, render it, fire the API call in the background. Success marks the row confirmed; failure reverts with a toast. Humans perceive sub-100ms as instant; anything above feels broken. Optimism is how you buy that latency back.`],quote:`If your ViewModel imports the HTTP client, your architecture is already leaking.`,table:{headers:[`Pattern`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`ViewModel → API direct`,`Fewest moving parts`,`No offline, untestable, per-screen ad hoc logic`,`Prototypes only`],[`Repository read-through`,`One data door, cacheable, testable`,`Writes still feel network-slow`,`Read-heavy apps`],[`Repository + optimistic writes`,`Sub-100ms perceived latency, offline mutations`,`Revert UX and conflict resolution needed`,`Anything with user actions`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Mobile Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-mobile-architecture.html`}},{heading:`03 · The network will betray you: backoff, keys, and OS-granted windows`,body:[`Now the part that cost us 412 double charges. The network will drop mid-request — not maybe, always. So retries are table stakes: OkHttp interceptors, URLSession background configuration, Ktor's retry plugin, all doing exponential backoff for you. But a retry without an idempotency key isn't resilience — it's a second charge. Every mutating request carries a key the server dedupes on. That one header would have saved the launch.`,`Background sync is the other surprise: you don't decide when it runs — the OS does. iOS hands you BGAppRefreshTask, roughly 30 seconds every few hours, when the OS feels like it. Android's WorkManager is constraint-driven: you express 'unmetered network, charging' and the OS schedules. Express constraints, surrender control.`,`So design sync like the window might be your only one: small payloads, resumable cursors, an outbox for pending mutations. If your sync takes four minutes, iOS will kill it at thirty seconds and you'll never figure out why the data's stale.`],quote:`Every request fails at least once — write the retry and the idempotency key before you write the request.`,table:{headers:[`Mutation strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Naive retry on failure`,`One line of code`,`Double charges, duplicate posts`,`Never in production`],[`Retry + idempotency key`,`Safe under any retry storm`,`Server must dedupe`,`Default for all writes`],[`Outbox queue + background sync`,`Survives app death mid-flight`,`Most state to manage`,`Offline-first mutations`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Mobile Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-mobile-architecture.html`}},{heading:`04 · Shipping under a warden: lifecycle, flags, and the review queue`,body:[`Last thought, and it's the one nobody budgets for: you don't control the device or the release. The OS rations CPU, kills your process in the background, and hands you lifecycle callbacks that decide whether state survives. Treat process death as the default case, not the exception — save the cart on background, restore on cold start.`,`And when you find a bug? Your hotfix enters a review queue with a multi-day SLA, and a chunk of users will install it next month or never. That's why the non-negotiables include crash reporting wired to release health, and server-driven feature flags that double as kill switches. Phased rollout — 1%, 10%, 100% — with a flag under every risky path means the blast radius of a bad build is a flag flip, not a six-day review wait.`,`'Can't we just test harder?' No — your fleet is a distributed system you don't operate: thousands of device models, OS versions, carriers, and clocks. You don't prevent every failure; you build the app so failures are survivable and reversible.`],quote:`Ship the kill switch before you ship the feature.`,table:{headers:[`Release strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Big-bang release`,`Simple, everyone gets it at once`,`Full blast radius, fixes bound by review queue`,`Nobody, honestly`],[`Phased rollout + crash gating`,`Halt at 1% signal, not 100% pain`,`Slower full availability`,`Default for every release`],[`Server-driven flags as kill switches`,`Instant revert without review`,`Flag debt if ungoverned`,`Every risky path`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Mobile Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-mobile-architecture.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`layered-architecture`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Offline-first layered architecture`,caption:`The UI observes the local DB; the repository is the only door to network and storage; the OS — not you — schedules the sync.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph L1["UI Layer - SwiftUI / Compose"]
        V["View"] --> VM["ViewModel"]
    end
    VM -->|"observes Flow / Combine"| DB[("Local DB<br/>SQLite / Room / GRDB")]
    VM -->|"user intent"| UC["Use Cases<br/>framework-free domain"]
    UC --> Repo["Repository<br/>the only data door"]
    Repo --> DB
    Repo --> API["Network Client<br/>OkHttp / URLSession"]
    API --> BE[("Backend API")]
    SYNC["Background Sync<br/>WorkManager / BGTask"] --> Repo
    FLAGS["Feature Flags<br/>server-driven"] -.->|"kill switch"| V
    CRASH["Crash Reporting<br/>release health"] -.-> VM`},{id:`optimistic-mutation`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Optimistic mutation with idempotent retry`,caption:`The tap writes locally in under 100ms; the network confirms or reverts in the background; the idempotency key makes every retry safe.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant U as User
    participant VM as ViewModel
    participant DB as Local DB
    participant R as Repository
    participant API as Backend

    U->>VM: Tap Confirm order
    VM->>DB: Write order, status=pending, idempotency key
    DB-->>VM: Row change emitted
    VM-->>U: Success UI in under 100ms
    R->>API: POST /orders with Idempotency-Key
    alt flaky network
        API--xR: timeout
        R->>R: backoff 1s, 2s, 4s
        R->>API: retry, same key
    end
    API-->>R: 201 Created, deduped by key
    R->>DB: status=confirmed
    Note over U,DB: Reactive stream repaints UI, no manual notify
    alt retries exhausted
        R->>DB: status=failed
        VM-->>U: Revert row plus toast
    end`}],takeaways:[`Make the local DB the single source of truth — every screen reads from Room/GRDB/SwiftData and never blocks on a round trip; offline is the default case, not the edge case.`,`Render mutations optimistically in under 100ms (the perceived-instant threshold), then confirm or revert from the background call with a toast.`,`Put an idempotency key on every mutating request and retry with exponential backoff (1s → 2s → 4s) — assume each request fails at least once.`,`Size background sync to the OS's budget: iOS BGAppRefreshTask gives roughly 30 seconds every few hours and Android WorkManager is constraint-driven — keep payloads small, resumable, and finishable in one window.`,`Ship a kill switch with every feature: server-driven flags plus phased rollout (1% → 10% → 100%), because app-store review can hold your hotfix for days.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Mobile Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-mobile-architecture.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-micro-frontends`,pathId:`the-modern-stack`,order:2,title:`Micro Frontends`,subtitle:`Multiple teams shipping into one UI shell, each owning a slice. Module federation, single-spa, web components. Most of the time it's overkill. The cases where it's right are real b`,minutes:10,difficulty:4.5,tags:[`Module federation`,`iframes`,`single-spa`],story:{headline:`2:47 AM, Black Friday week: the checkout surface went white, and 'revert' had no meaning.`,paragraphs:[`Six teams, one fintech dashboard, one shell. After a conference talk, the platform lead green-lit module federation: every team ships its own remote, deploys on its own cadence, no more release train. For four months it was genuinely great — PRs got small, CI got fast, and the demo at the all-hands got applause.`,`Then, Black Friday week, the payments team upgraded to React 19 behind what they thought was an isolated build. The host was on React 18. Module federation's shared-singleton negotiation resolved the wrong copy at runtime, every hook call in the checkout remote threw 'Invalid hook call', and the revenue path white-screened at 2:47 AM. Worse: 'just revert' had no meaning — each MFE deploys independently, and nobody could say which remote had changed in the last six hours.`,`The fix was boring. Pin React to one major org-wide, treat the design system version like an API contract, write down who owns the shell. And one honest postmortem line: the original pain was two teams blocking each other's releases — a shared package and a weekly sync would have cost a tenth of the federation setup.`],problem:`Multiple teams shipping independent surfaces of one product at different cadences — without one team's deploy white-screening another team's revenue path.`},tech:{stack:[`Module Federation`,`single-spa`,`Iframes`,`Monorepo packages`,`Shared singletons`],note:`The composition machinery this lesson lives in: build-time monorepo packages, run-time federation and single-spa, and the iframe sandbox — plus the shared-singleton layer where the outages happen.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'microservices for the browser' fails`,body:[`Let me start where every MFE journey starts: pain. One frontend codebase, many teams. Conflicting library versions, slow CI, PRs that touch everyone's code, release coupling — team A's bug blocks team B's launch. If that's your life, micro frontends sound like salvation.`,`But here's the uncomfortable data point: most front-end orgs that adopt them regret it within 18 months. Three taxes show up on the invoice. The duplication tax — each MFE ships its own React copy. The consistency tax — every team's Button looks slightly different. The orchestration tax — who deploys the shell, and what happens when the shell and a remote disagree?`,`So my first move is always the boring one: a monorepo with shared packages — Nx, Turborepo. Solve the problem with build-time composition first. If it still hurts after a quarter, then we talk about federation.`],quote:`The Module Federation demo looks great. The Module Federation production system looks like a service mesh in HTML.`,table:{headers:[`Tax`,`What it costs`,`When it bites`],rows:[[`Duplication`,`~50 KB gzipped React per MFE`,`Every page load`],[`Consistency`,`N slightly-different Buttons`,`First user complaint`],[`Orchestration`,`Shell + N remotes to coordinate`,`Every deploy day`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Micro Frontends`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-micro-frontends.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: three ways to compose`,body:[`There are exactly three ways to compose micro frontends, and the choice is really about trust. Build-time: each team's UI is a package in a monorepo; CI builds the whole thing. Simplest, best for most teams — and by most definitions not 'micro frontends' at all. It's the alternative you should pick.`,`Run-time: Webpack Module Federation (or the Vite plugin, or RSPack) lets the host load remote modules from URLs. Each MFE deploys independently; the host picks up the new version on the next page load. The cost: shared dependencies get resolved per page load, and sharing types across repos gets genuinely tricky. single-spa is the other run-time animal — a framework-of-frameworks that mounts React, Vue, and Angular into one page. Powerful, scary, and best suited to incremental migrations, not greenfield design.`,`And the oldest trick: iframes. Separate JS context, scoped CSS, a real security boundary — perfect for untrusted third-party content like payment widgets. You pay with awkward cross-frame communication and harder deep linking.`],quote:`Pick your composition mode by how much you trust the other teams: build-time for full trust, federation for negotiated trust, iframes for none.`,table:{headers:[`Mode`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Build-time (monorepo)`,`Simple CI, one deploy`,`Coupled releases`,`1-3 teams, high trust`],[`Module federation`,`Independent deploys`,`Runtime dep negotiation`,`3+ teams, negotiated trust`],[`single-spa`,`Mix frameworks`,`Complex lifecycle`,`Incremental migrations`],[`Iframes`,`Hard isolation`,`Awkward comms, deep links`,`Untrusted third parties`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Micro Frontends`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-micro-frontends.html`}},{heading:`03 · The shared dependency trap`,body:[`Now the trap behind my 2:47 AM call. React is about 50 KB gzipped. If each of ten MFEs ships its own copy, that's 500 KB before your first line of app code. Module federation's answer is the shared singleton: declare React once, everyone uses the host's instance.`,`But notice what just happened: a build-time concern became a runtime contract. The version-matching rules are subtle, and one MFE on React 18 next to another on React 19 breaks hooks — at runtime, in production, in front of users. That is exactly the outage from the story.`,`Same story with the design system. If every MFE has its own Button, the product looks like five products. So you centralise it, version it — and now publishing v2 means coordinating with N MFE teams. Which is the release coupling micro frontends were supposed to remove. Sharing a dependency is easy; sharing the decision to upgrade it is the hard part.`],quote:`Sharing a dependency is easy. Sharing the decision to upgrade it is the hard part.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Bundle cost`,`Coordination cost`,`Failure mode`],rows:[[`Each MFE ships its own`,`~50 KB per MFE`,`None`,`Version drift, broken hooks`],[`Shared singleton`,`One copy`,`Org-wide version pinning`,`Runtime negotiation failure`],[`Design system as API`,`One copy`,`Release train per major`,`Visual drift if skipped`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Micro Frontends`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-micro-frontends.html`}},{heading:`04 · When I actually say yes`,body:[`So when do I actually say yes? When the problem is organisational and specific: multiple teams shipping independent surfaces of one product, at genuinely different cadences. Not 'we want microservices for the front-end' — that sentence has never once preceded a happy outcome.`,`Two green lights I trust. First: your monorepo has stopped being able to ship — CI is the bottleneck, teams are stepping on each other's releases, and a shared-package refactor can't fix it. Second: incremental migration — single-spa exists to strangle a legacy monolith one route at a time. And iframes whenever you embed code you don't control: payment widgets, third-party tools.`,`Everything else? Build-time composition and a weekly sync. The demo is easy; the production system is a service mesh in HTML. Make sure the org pain is real before you buy the mesh.`],quote:`Micro frontends are the answer when your monorepo has stopped being able to ship. Not before.`,table:{headers:[`Signal`,`Right answer`],rows:[[`1-2 teams, one product`,`Monorepo + packages`],[`3+ teams, blocked releases`,`Module federation`],[`Legacy framework migration`,`single-spa strangler`],[`Third-party / untrusted UI`,`Iframe sandbox`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Micro Frontends`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-micro-frontends.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Runtime composition topology — and where it drifts`,caption:`The shell loads remotes at runtime and negotiates shared singletons per page load. The stale design system and the stray React 19 copy are exactly how the 2:47 AM outage happened.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    U["User browser"] --> H["Shell host app"]
    H -->|"runtime fetch"| C["Checkout MFE"]
    H -->|"runtime fetch"| S["Search MFE"]
    H -->|"runtime fetch"| A["Admin MFE"]
    C --> DS["Design system v2"]
    S --> DS
    A --> DS1["Design system v1 stale"]
    H -.->|"shared singleton"| R["React 18 instance"]
    C -.-> R
    S -.-> R
    A -.-> R19["React 19 own copy"]`},{id:`load-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Page load under Module Federation`,caption:`Shared-dependency negotiation happens on every page load — a major-version mismatch surfaces here, at runtime, in front of users.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant B as Browser
    participant H as Shell host
    participant CDN as Remote entry CDN
    B->>H: GET /dashboard
    H->>CDN: fetch remoteEntry.js (checkout MFE)
    CDN-->>H: manifest + exposed modules
    H->>H: negotiate shared deps (React singleton)
    alt versions compatible
        H->>B: mount checkout surface
    else major version mismatch
        H->>B: Invalid hook call - white screen
    end`}],takeaways:[`Single team, single product: don't. A monorepo with shared packages ships faster than federation you'll spend months tuning — reach for MFEs only when 3+ teams are blocking each other's releases.`,`Budget the duplication tax: each MFE shipping its own React adds ~50 KB gzipped — 10 MFEs is 500 KB before a single line of your app code.`,`Pin shared singletons to one major version org-wide: a React 18 host resolving a React 19 remote throws 'Invalid hook call' at runtime, not at build time.`,`Version the design system like an API: every major bump is a coordinated release across N MFE teams — put it on a calendar, or teams drift within weeks.`,`Use iframes when embedding untrusted code (payment widgets, third-party tools): you buy a hard security boundary at the cost of awkward cross-frame messaging and harder deep linking.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Micro Frontends`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-micro-frontends.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-sidecar`,pathId:`the-modern-stack`,order:3,title:`Sidecar Pattern`,subtitle:`The sidecar pattern co-locates a helper process with your application — same pod, same lifecycle, separate process. Logging, tracing, mTLS, service discovery, secret rotation, retr`,minutes:10,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`Dapr`,`Envoy`,`Linkerd`,`OpenTelemetry Collector`],story:{headline:`Three retry loops walked into a blackout.`,paragraphs:[`2:47 AM. A routine deploy on the payments dependency blips for forty seconds. Routine. Should be a non-event. But when the dependency came back up, it got slammed by a synchronized wall of retries and fell over again — and this time it took checkout down with it for twenty-two minutes.`,`The postmortem read like a polyglot crime scene. The Java service had hand-rolled exponential backoff with jitter — actually fine. The Node service used a retry library pinned two majors back: fixed one-second intervals, zero jitter, so every client fired at the same instant. The Go service's retry config lived in a repo whose author had left the company; nobody knew it was configured to retry zero times. Three languages, three retry implementations, three different behaviors under failure — and the only coordinated thing about them was the outage.`,`The fix wasn't writing better retry code in three languages. It was deleting retry code from three languages. We put an Envoy sidecar next to every pod, moved retries, timeouts, circuit breaking, and mTLS into it, and the apps went back to doing the one thing they were good at. The next upstream blip, months later, was a Slack message and a shrug: the sidecars rode it out, and the applications never even saw the 503s.`],problem:`Every service was reimplementing the same cross-cutting concerns — retries, TLS, telemetry — in its own language, its own way, with its own bugs. The pattern that fixed it: co-locate one helper process per pod that owns those concerns once, for every app that lives beside it.`},tech:{stack:[`Envoy`,`Linkerd`,`Dapr`,`OTel Collector`,`Kubernetes Pods`,`Istio Ambient`,`Cilium eBPF`],note:`One helper process per pod owns the cross-cutting concerns — proxying, mTLS, retries, telemetry — while the app container keeps only business logic. Linkerd and Istio are this pattern deployed fleet-wide; Dapr and the OTel Collector are the same idea for portable APIs and observability.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just a shared library?`,body:[`So the obvious move is: write the retry logic once, publish it as a library, everyone imports it. I've lived this. It works beautifully for about six months. Then service A is pinned to 2.3 because upgrading means running a regression suite nobody owns, service B is on 3.1, and service C is in a language the library doesn't speak at all. Congratulations — you've built a distributed monolith held together by version skew.`,`Worse, every library upgrade becomes a fleet-wide coordinated event. That's exactly the coupling microservices were supposed to eliminate. Option two is a central gateway: one place to configure everything. Great for ingress — useless for service-to-service hops, which is precisely where retries, mTLS, and circuit breaking actually matter.`,`The sidecar is the third answer: put the logic in a small process next to every pod. The app talks plain HTTP over localhost, so it doesn't even need a TLS library. You upgrade the sidecar image with a rolling deploy, completely decoupled from application release trains. Polyglot by construction.`],quote:`A library couples your upgrade calendar to every team's. A sidecar decouples it.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Shared library`,`No extra process; lowest latency`,`Version skew, polyglot tax, coordinated upgrades`,`Monoglot shop, small fleet`],[`Central gateway`,`One place to configure policy`,`Edge only; blind to internal east-west hops`,`Ingress / north-south traffic`],[`Sidecar per pod`,`Language-agnostic; fleet-wide upgrades; per-pod policy`,`~50–100 MB per pod; extra localhost hop`,`East-west service-to-service traffic`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Sidecar Pattern`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-sidecar.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what you outsource, what you keep`,body:[`The whole design hangs on drawing this line correctly. Outsource everything that's the same shape in every service: TLS termination and mTLS, retries, timeouts, circuit breaking, log shipping, trace export, config reload, secret rotation. Keep everything that's genuinely yours: domain logic, business rules, the code that would make no sense pasted into another service.`,`Here's the test I actually use in design reviews: would this code be byte-for-byte identical if I copy-pasted it into a different service? If yes, it's sidecar material. If it contains your domain, it stays in the app. Teams get this wrong in both directions — I've seen people put request validation in the sidecar (now your business rules live in YAML) and I've seen people hand-roll mTLS cert rotation in application code (now your security posture lives in a PR queue).`,`The boundary itself is beautifully simple: localhost. The app makes plain HTTP calls to 127.0.0.1 and the sidecar does the ugly parts on the way out the door. That's why the pattern is language-agnostic — every language since the dawn of sockets can make a localhost call.`],quote:`Outsource the boring problems. Keep the interesting ones.`,table:{headers:[`Outsource to the sidecar`,`Keep in the app`],rows:[[`mTLS & cert rotation`,`Domain logic & business rules`],[`Retries, timeouts, circuit breaking`,`Business transactions & workflows`],[`Log shipping & trace export`,`Request validation & authz decisions`],[`Config reload & secret delivery`,`Anything you'd write twice`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Sidecar Pattern`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-sidecar.html`}},{heading:`03 · The lifecycle contract: one pod, one fate`,body:[`What makes this a sidecar and not just 'two containers that happen to chat'? The lifecycle contract. Same pod means: shared network namespace — that's why localhost works — optionally shared volumes for secrets and logs, and one deployment, scale, and failure domain. They are not two services that talk; they are one operational unit with two processes.`,`The ordering matters more than people think, and it's where most sidecar outages actually come from. If the app starts before the proxy is listening, the first requests black-hole. If the proxy dies before the app drains, you drop in-flight requests on every single rollout. Kubernetes used to make you hack this with init containers and postStart sleeps; since 1.29 native sidecar containers — init containers with restartPolicy: Always — give you the real contract: the proxy starts first, stays restarted if it crashes, and terminates last.`,`Get this right and the sidecar disappears from your operators' mental model. Get it wrong and every deploy is a small outage lottery — which is exactly the kind of thing that pages you at 3 AM.`],quote:`Deployed together, scaled together, failed together — a sidecar is half of one operational unit, not a second service.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Sidecar Pattern`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-sidecar.html`}},{heading:`04 · The bill: sidecars aren't free`,body:[`Let's be honest about the cost, because this is where the pattern is being actively reinvented. Every pod now runs two processes. Realistically that's 50–100 MB of memory and a fraction of a core per sidecar. Now do the fleet math: 10,000 pods times 75 MB is roughly 750 GB of RAM spent on proxies before you've served a single request. Plus two extra TCP hops and typically half a millisecond to two milliseconds of p99 per call.`,`That bill is why the frontier moved. Cilium pushes proxying into eBPF at the kernel — no extra process at all. Istio Ambient mode replaces the per-pod Envoy with a per-node ztunnel for L4 and mTLS, plus optional waypoint proxies when you need L7 smarts. Same feature set, amortized cost. This is the live debate in service-mesh design right now.`,`But sidecarless isn't a free lunch either. Per-node proxies trade per-pod isolation for shared fate on the node, and the L7 features still drag you back to waypoints anyway. I still reach for per-pod sidecars when teams want hard workload isolation or zero-trust enforced at the pod boundary — sometimes the tax is the product.`],quote:`At one pod, the sidecar is free. At ten thousand pods, it's a datacenter.`,table:{headers:[`Deployment shape`,`Overhead`,`Isolation`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Per-pod sidecar`,`~50–100 MB + 0.1–0.5 vCPU per pod`,`Per-pod; strongest`,`Istio classic, Linkerd`],[`Per-node proxy`,`Amortized across the node`,`Per-node; shared fate`,`Istio Ambient ztunnel`],[`Kernel (eBPF)`,`Lowest; no extra process`,`Node-level`,`Cilium`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Sidecar Pattern`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-sidecar.html`}},{heading:`05 · When I reach for one — and when I don't`,body:[`Here's my decision heuristic after living with these things for years. Reach for a sidecar when the concern is identical across services — retries, mTLS, telemetry — when the fleet is polyglot, or when you need to enforce policy without filing PRs against every app team. Don't bother when you have three services in one language (a library is cheaper), when the concern is genuinely domain-specific, or when you're so latency-critical that two extra hops break the budget.`,`And notice the pattern generalizes far beyond proxies. Dapr is the same idea one level up: the sidecar exposes portable APIs for state, pub/sub, bindings, and secrets, so your app talks to 'the sidecar API' instead of importing the Redis SDK and the Kafka SDK and the Azure SDK. The OpenTelemetry Collector as a sidecar does it for observability: app emits OTLP to localhost, the collector handles batching, retries, and export. Same shape every time — app speaks localhost, sidecar speaks the world.`],quote:`If three services need it and two languages speak it, it belongs in a sidecar.`,table:{headers:[`Use case`,`Tool`,`What the app sees`],rows:[[`Retries, mTLS, routing`,`Envoy / Linkerd`,`Plain HTTP on localhost`],[`Traces & metrics export`,`OTel Collector`,`OTLP to localhost`],[`State, pub/sub, secrets`,`Dapr`,`Sidecar HTTP/gRPC API`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Sidecar Pattern`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-sidecar.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`pod-anatomy`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of a sidecar pod`,caption:`One pod, one network namespace, two processes. The app only ever speaks plain HTTP over localhost; the sidecar handles everything facing the network.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph Pod["One pod — shared network namespace"]
    direction LR
    App["App container<br/>business logic only"]
    SC["Sidecar proxy<br/>mTLS · retries · telemetry"]
    SC -->|"forward on localhost :8080"| App
    App -->|"dial localhost :15001"| SC
  end
  Caller["Upstream caller"] -->|"mTLS in :15006"| SC
  SC -->|"mTLS · retries · circuit breaking"| Down["Downstream service"]
  SC -.->|"logs · traces · metrics"| Tel["OTel Collector"]`},{id:`retry-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The blackout, replayed with a sidecar`,caption:`The same 3 AM blip, but the retry policy lives in the proxy. The application never learns the dependency hiccupped — no synchronized retry walls, no thundering herd.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant App as App container
  participant SC as Local sidecar
  participant RSC as Remote sidecar
  participant Dep as Payment API
  App->>SC: POST /pay (plain HTTP, localhost)
  SC->>RSC: request over mTLS
  RSC->>Dep: forward
  Dep-->>RSC: 503 (40s blip)
  RSC-->>SC: 503
  Note over SC: sidecar policy: max 3 attempts,<br/>jittered exponential backoff, 5xx only
  SC->>RSC: retry #2 after ~100ms
  RSC->>Dep: forward
  Dep-->>RSC: 200 OK
  RSC-->>SC: 200 OK
  SC-->>App: 200 OK
  Note over App: app never saw the failure`}],takeaways:[`Budget ~50–100 MB RAM and 0.1–0.5 vCPU per sidecar; at 10,000 pods that's 0.5–1 TB of memory for proxies alone — run the fleet math before you mesh everything.`,`The 3-and-2 rule: if a concern needs identical config in 3+ services or 2+ languages, move it to a sidecar; if it lives in one service, keep it in-process.`,`Cap sidecar retries at 2–3 attempts with jittered exponential backoff on 5xx/connection failures only — and never retry a non-idempotent POST without an idempotency key.`,`Use Kubernetes native sidecar containers (init container with restartPolicy: Always, on by default since 1.29) so the proxy starts before the app and drains after it — that ordering eliminates dropped requests during every rollout.`,`Measure the tax: a sidecar adds two extra TCP hops and typically ~0.5–2 ms p99 per call; if that breaks your latency budget, evaluate sidecarless meshes (Cilium eBPF, Istio Ambient ztunnel).`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Sidecar Pattern`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-sidecar.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-deployment`,pathId:`the-modern-stack`,order:4,title:`Deployment Patterns`,subtitle:`Five deployment patterns, ordered by how much risk they take with users. Rolling is the default; canary is the safety net; blue-green is the rollback insurance; feature flags decou`,minutes:10,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`Argo Rollouts`,`Flagger`,`feature flags`],story:{headline:`3:07 AM: the rolling deploy rolled straight over checkout.`,paragraphs:[`It's 3:07 AM when the pager fires. A completely routine rolling deploy of the payments service — a boring 'add retry logic' change — is halfway through swapping pods when checkout error rates start climbing. The new v2 pods write refund records with a fresh status enum value; the v1 pods still serving half the traffic have never heard of that value and answer with 500s. Every pod the rollout swaps makes the mix worse, not better.`,`The on-call engineer does the textbook thing: roll back. Except rollback here is just... another rolling deploy. Ten more minutes of mixed versions while the error rate keeps ticking, and the poisoned refund records are already in the database — v1 can't read them no matter how many pods you run. By the time the fleet is back on v1, checkout has been degraded for 23 minutes and support has 400 tickets.`,`The postmortem verdict wasn't 'be more careful at 3 AM.' It was that the team had exactly one lever — deploy — and it was wired directly to 100% of users. No canary slice to burn first, no parallel stack to flip back to, no flag to let the new code sit dark in production. The fix wasn't a process doc. It was buying the ability to have code live in production that no user can see.`],problem:`How do you ship changes without betting every user on the whole deploy at once — and how do you separate 'code reached production' from 'users can see it'?`},tech:{stack:[`Kubernetes`,`Argo Rollouts`,`Flagger`,`Prometheus`,`Feature Flags`,`Blue-Green`],note:`Rollout controllers drive the ramp, Prometheus analysis calls green or red, and the flag layer splits deploy from release — together they turn a deploy from one bet into a dial.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: every deploy is a bet — who holds the ticket?`,body:[`Let me start with the uncomfortable framing: every deploy is a hypothesis — 'this version is no worse than the last one' — and you only find out whether it holds after users are already on it. So the only real design question is: how many users are holding the ticket when the hypothesis fails? That's it. That's the entire decision.`,`You pick the pattern by the cost of being wrong. If the failure mode is a 5% conversion dip on the homepage, a canary with automated analysis catches it while 99% of users are still safe. If the failure mode is three minutes of static error pages during a quiet window, honestly, rolling is fine. The mistake teams make is running one pattern everywhere — paying blue-green's 2× capacity tax on typo fixes, or rolling a payments rewrite out to everyone at once. Ask: what does 'wrong' cost, and who pays it?`],quote:`You don't roll back. You roll forward — to the version that was working ten minutes ago.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Deployment Patterns`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-deployment.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the five patterns, out loud`,body:[`Climb the ladder with me. Recreate: kill the old version, start the new one — downtime by design, fine for a weekend internal tool, never for production. Rolling: Kubernetes' default — spin up one v2 pod, wait for readiness, kill one v1 pod, repeat. Capacity stays roughly constant, it's fast and cheap, but users hit a mix of v1 and v2 mid-rollout. Hold onto that mix — it's exactly what bit us at 3 AM.`,`Blue-green: keep v1 running, deploy v2 to identical capacity alongside, then flip traffic at the load balancer atomically. Rollback is one config change. The price is 2× capacity during the cutover window — spend it on schema migrations and framework upgrades, the deploys you suspect might surprise you. Canary: route 1–5% of traffic to v2, watch the metrics, expand on green, halt on red. Argo Rollouts and Flagger automate that whole ramp-halt loop against Prometheus. A/B testing is canary's cousin with a different question — not 'is v2 broken?' but 'does v2 win?' — sticky per-user splits, product metrics, hours to weeks.`,`Notice there's no winner on this list. There's a portfolio. Most healthy orgs run rolling for the boring 90% of deploys and reach for canary or blue-green when the change is scary.`],quote:`There's no best deployment pattern — there's a portfolio, matched to what each failure costs.`,table:{headers:[`Pattern`,`Blast radius`,`Capacity cost`,`Rollback speed`,`Reach for it when`],rows:[[`Recreate`,`100% of users`,`None`,`N/A — downtime`,`Internal tools, never prod`],[`Rolling`,`Mixed v1/v2 mid-rollout`,`~+1 pod`,`Another full cycle (~10 min)`,`Routine, low-risk deploys`],[`Blue-green`,`Atomic switch, all-or-nothing`,`2× during cutover`,`Seconds — one LB flip`,`Schema migrations, big upgrades`],[`Canary`,`1–5% of users first`,`~+10% canary pods`,`Seconds — shrink to 0`,`Risky user-facing changes`],[`A/B test`,`Sticky random cohorts`,`Same as canary`,`Flag flip`,`Product metric decisions`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Deployment Patterns`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-deployment.html`}},{heading:`03 · Why not just roll back?`,body:[`Here's the part nobody puts on the architecture diagram: the patterns differ mostly in how fast you can say 'undo.' Rolling rollback is... another rolling deploy — ten more minutes of mixed versions while you're already bleeding. Blue-green rollback is one load-balancer flip: seconds. Canary rollback is shrinking the canary to zero: also seconds, and the analysis loop does it for you before a human even wakes up.`,`And then there's the trap that got us at 3 AM: rollback doesn't undo data. v2 had already written refund records with a status enum v1 couldn't parse. This is why schema changes want expand/contract — add the new column and dual-write first, deploy readers that tolerate both shapes, and only contract after every version that has seen the data is gone. Your rollback speed is a design decision you make weeks before the deploy, not during the incident.`],quote:`Rollback speed is a design decision — you make it weeks before the deploy, not during the incident.`,table:{headers:[`Pattern`,`How you undo`,`Time to undo`,`The catch`],rows:[[`Rolling`,`Redeploy v1, full cycle`,`~5–15 min`,`Mixed versions again mid-undo`],[`Blue-green`,`Flip LB back to blue`,`Seconds`,`Blue must stay warm`],[`Canary`,`Scale canary to 0`,`Seconds`,`Only automatic if analysis is wired up`],[`Feature flag`,`Flip flag off`,`< 1 min, zero pods`,`Code stays deployed, just dark`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Deployment Patterns`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-deployment.html`}},{heading:`04 · Deploy ≠ release: the flag layer`,body:[`The fifth pattern isn't a deployment strategy at all — it's the realisation that deploy and release are two different events. Ship v2 with the new checkout wrapped in if (flag.IsEnabled('new-checkout')). Roll v2 to 100% of pods. The code is in production; the flag is off; users still see the old checkout. Days or weeks later, turn the flag on for 1%, then 10%, then 100% — and if it burns, the rollback is a config change that touches zero pods.`,`This is the single highest-leverage operational change a team can make. Deploys become routine because they stop being releases. A/B tests are just flag variants with a measurement loop. Long migrations ship dark and turn on per-cohort. Flickr invented this in 2009, and every org that ships fast today runs on it. The one tax: flag debt. Every flag is a branch in your code someone has to remember exists — delete them within weeks of full rollout, or the maze wins.`],quote:`The deploy is for your pipeline. The release is for your users. Stop wiring them together.`,table:{headers:[`Flag stage`,`Who sees v2`,`Undo move`],rows:[[`Off (dark ship)`,`Nobody — code deployed, dormant`,`Nothing needed`],[`Internal / 1%`,`Employees, then a tiny cohort`,`Flip flag off — instant`],[`10 → 50%`,`Growing random cohorts`,`Flip flag off — instant`],[`100%, flag removed`,`Everyone`,`Only a real rollback now`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Deployment Patterns`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-deployment.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`canary-loop`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Automated canary: the ramp–halt–rollback loop`,caption:`Argo Rollouts or Flagger drive the ramp against Prometheus analysis; red metrics collapse the canary to zero before most users ever notice.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["v2 image passes CI"] --> B["Argo Rollouts starts canary at 1%"]
    B --> C["Prometheus: error rate, p99, saturation"]
    C --> D{"Analysis run: within SLO?"}
    D -->|green| E["Ramp to 10%"]
    E --> C
    D -->|red| F["Halt rollout"]
    F --> G["Scale canary to 0 — v1 serves 100%"]
    E --> H["Ramp 10 → 50 → 100%"]
    H --> I["v1 scaled down — rollout complete"]`},{id:`blue-green-seq`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Blue-green cutover and the one-flip rollback`,caption:`Green runs at full capacity and passes smoke tests before the load balancer ever points at it; rollback is a single upstream flip back to blue.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant CD as CI/CD Pipeline
    participant LB as Load Balancer
    participant B as v1 blue
    participant G as v2 green
    CD->>G: Deploy v2 at full capacity
    G->>G: Smoke tests on green only
    Note over LB,B: Before cutover — 100% of traffic on blue
    CD->>LB: Flip upstream to green
    LB->>G: 100% of user traffic
    Note over CD,G: Rollback = one config change
    CD->>LB: Flip back to blue
    LB->>B: 100% of traffic again`}],takeaways:[`Default to rolling for ~90% of routine deploys — it's the Kubernetes default, costs ~1 extra pod of headroom, and nobody should pay blue-green's 2× capacity tax for a typo fix.`,`Canary recipe: start at 1–5%, hold each step 10–15 minutes, auto-halt if error rate exceeds baseline by >0.5–1% or p99 regresses >10%.`,`Blue-green buys seconds-level rollback at 2× capacity during cutover — reserve it for schema migrations and framework upgrades, and keep blue warm.`,`Wrap risky user-facing changes in a feature flag: deploy daily, release weekly, and keep the kill switch under a 60-second flip that touches zero pods.`,`Budget flag debt: delete each flag within ~30 days of 100% rollout, or every future deploy wades through dead branches.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Deployment Patterns`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-deployment.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`mj-WQFx2m5Ub9M`,note:`Docker Compose with .NET 8, PostgreSQL, and Redis (step by step) covers this from a different angle.`},{id:`mj-SI38jyEiso4`,note:`Deploy Full-Stack .NET Apps for FREE in 2025 (Azure + Postgres) covers this from a different angle.`},{id:`mj--73fAqw8ckU`,note:`Migrating From Docker Compose to .NET Aspire (my experience) covers this from a different angle.`}],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-cloud-pitfalls`,pathId:`the-modern-stack`,order:5,title:`15 Cloud Pitfalls`,subtitle:`Fifteen pitfalls that turn into Sev-2s, surprise bills, and resume-paragraph stories. None are exotic. All are documented. Most production teams ship at least three of them by acci`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`RAG`],story:{headline:`The night the bill, the outage, and the leak all showed up together`,paragraphs:[`03:12. AZ-b goes dark and checkout dies with it. The on-call engineer opens the runbook and finds one line: 'database failover is manual.' Manual how? The person who knew left two quarters ago. The standby exists, replication is healthy, and still nothing promotes — because nobody ever rehearsed the moment the standby becomes the primary.`,`While she fights the fire, two more landmines go off. Scaling the app tier into a healthy AZ hits a vCPU quota nobody knew existed — the incident is now blocked on a support ticket. And that same morning, finance flags the month's bill: six times normal, driven by a NAT gateway chewing on dev-environment traffic, cross-AZ chatter between an app tier and a database in the wrong AZ, and a reserved-instance commitment that quietly expired.`,`The postmortem finds the dessert: an S3 bucket of customer exports, world-readable since March, sitting next to an IAM role with star-star on everything. None of it exotic. All of it documented. Three pitfalls — cost, reliability, security — shipped by accident in a single quarter, by a competent team that read the docs they had time to read.`],problem:`Cloud defaults are tuned for the five-minute demo, not the five-year system — so cost, reliability, and security pitfalls ship silently until they become the 3 AM page.`},tech:{stack:[`Cross-AZ Egress`,`NAT Gateway`,`Multi-AZ Failover`,`S3 Public Access`,`Least-Privilege IAM`,`Billing Alerts`],note:`The lesson walks the four surfaces where cloud defaults bite: the meter (cross-AZ egress, NAT gateway), the outage (single-AZ databases, untested failover, quotas), the breach (public buckets, wildcard IAM, logged secrets), and the guardrails that inhibit all three.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does the bill read like a phone number?`,body:[`Let's start with the part nobody puts in the architecture diagram: the meter. Your app tier lives in AZ-a, your database in AZ-b, and every single call between them crosses a fiber span the provider meters at roughly a cent per gigabyte, each direction. It looks free in the console. It is not free on the invoice. And here's the trap — the topology was decided on day one in your IaC, so the distance tax started accruing before anyone thought about cost.`,`Then there's the NAT gateway, the toll booth nobody drew. It idles at about thirty-three dollars a month before it moves a single byte, then charges again per gigabyte processed. Add the classics — a dev environment someone spun up in March and forgot, a reservation that quietly lapsed — and you've got a bill that doubles while traffic stays flat. Why not just 'optimize later'? Because 'later' is when finance finds it, and finance doesn't file tickets — it freezes your capacity requests.`],quote:`The cloud bills you for physics — distance is a line item.`,table:{headers:[`Leak`,`Cost shape`,`Fix`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Cross-AZ chatter`,`~$0.01/GB each way, every call`,`Co-locate hot paths in one AZ`,`Always`],[`NAT Gateway`,`~$33/mo idle + ~$0.045/GB processed`,`VPC endpoints for S3/API; audit egress`,`Most VPCs`],[`Forgotten dev envs`,`24/7 spend for 0% value`,`Tagging + auto-off + budget alerts`,`Every account`],[`Expired reservations`,`On-demand rates return silently`,`Renewal calendar + utilization reports`,`Steady workloads`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — 15 Cloud Pitfalls`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cloud-pitfalls.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the reliability boundary: what breaks when one building does`,body:[`Now flip to the night the lights go out. A single-AZ database is the pitfall that turns one building's bad night into your bad quarter. Multi-AZ is a checkbox in the console — but a checkbox you've never tested is a rumor. The standby exists, replication is flowing, and yet at 03:12 nobody knows who's allowed to promote, because the runbook says 'failover is manual' and the person who wrote it left in 2023.`,`Same blindness with backups: 'managed storage' means the provider runs it, not that they're keeping copies for you. An EBS volume is not snapshotted by magic, and backup retention is whatever you set — which is often 'whatever the default was'. And quotas, those invisible vCPU ceilings, only reveal themselves when you try to scale out during the incident. Why not trust the provider's automation? Because automation has preconditions — a healthy standby, quota headroom, DNS TTLs someone remembers to lower — and you discover every one of them at 03:12, not in a review doc.`,`Region failover is the same story one level up. Untested, it's fiction with a runbook. Tested twice a year, it's a muscle. The difference costs one game day per quarter and saves you the Sev-2.`],quote:`A failover you've never rehearsed is fiction with a runbook.`,table:{headers:[`Posture`,`What you get`,`What it costs`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single-AZ`,`Cheapest, simplest`,`AZ outage = full outage`,`Demos only`],[`Multi-AZ standby`,`Automatic failover, ~2x DB cost`,`Cross-AZ app calls still metered`,`Most production`],[`Multi-AZ + replicas`,`Read scale, faster recovery`,`More endpoints to guard`,`Read-heavy systems`],[`Cross-region passive`,`Survives region loss`,`Untested = fiction; data gravity`,`Regulated / global`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — 15 Cloud Pitfalls`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cloud-pitfalls.html`}},{heading:`03 · The convenience tax: public buckets, fat IAM, and logs that remember too much`,body:[`Third act: the leak. Public S3 buckets are the classic because 'public' is a default you have to actively undo — and undoing it bucket-by-bucket means one clickops mistake ships customer exports to the world. The fix isn't vigilance, it's making the wrong thing impossible: account-level Block Public Access, org policy, done once, everywhere.`,`Then IAM. A role with star-star feels convenient right up until one container escape becomes a full account compromise — the blast radius of wildcard permissions is the entire account. And logging: turn on verbose debug and your Authorization headers, signed URLs, and connection strings flow into a log store every on-call can read. Logs are a database of secrets you didn't mean to build.`,`Static service-account keys are the quiet one: a long-lived credential in a repo is a countdown clock, and the countdown ends when a scanner finds it. Workload identity and short-lived tokens exist precisely so the clock never starts.`],quote:`Public is a default you have to actively undo — so undo it once, at the org level.`,table:{headers:[`Default`,`Risk`,`Guardrail`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Public-by-mistake buckets`,`Data exposure, headline risk`,`Org-level Block Public Access`,`All accounts`],[`Wildcard IAM roles`,`One escape = account takeover`,`Scoped roles + access reviews`,`Every role`],[`Secrets in logs`,`Leak via the observability path`,`Redaction filters + scrub tests`,`All services`],[`Static service keys`,`Long-lived creds in repos`,`Workload identity, short-lived tokens`,`Wherever supported`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — 15 Cloud Pitfalls`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cloud-pitfalls.html`}},{heading:`04 · Why these ship anyway — and how to make the safe path the default`,body:[`So why do all fifteen ship anyway? Because the docs assume you'll read every page, and the defaults are tuned for the five-minute demo, not the five-year system. Detection — alerts, audits, cost reports — finds each pitfall after it has already billed you, paged you, or leaked you. Inhibition — org policies, policy-as-code in CI, deny-by-default — stops it before the first request ever fires.`,`The practical stack is boring on purpose: billing alarms at 50/80/100% of budget, a quarterly quota review, a failover game day twice a year, secret scanning on every push, and IaC templates that simply don't offer a public-bucket option. None of it is clever. All of it is the difference between 'the architecture' and 'the architecture review'. Your job as the designer is to make the safe path the path of least resistance — because at 3 AM, nobody resists anything.`],quote:`Guardrails beat checklists, because nobody reads a checklist at 3 AM.`,table:{headers:[`Control`,`Mode`,`Catches`,`Misses`],rows:[[`Billing alarms`,`Detect`,`Spend anomalies`,`Money already spent`],[`Policy-as-code in CI`,`Inhibit`,`Bad IaC at PR time`,`Clickops drift`],[`Failover game days`,`Detect + practice`,`Broken runbooks, quota walls`,`Costs a day per quarter`],[`Secret scanning`,`Detect`,`Committed keys`,`Secrets only ever logged`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — 15 Cloud Pitfalls`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cloud-pitfalls.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`money-and-outage-map`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Where the money leaks and the outage starts`,caption:`One VPC, two AZs: the cross-AZ hop is metered, the NAT gateway is a toll booth, and the bucket's public path bypasses all of it.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph VPC[One Region VPC]
    subgraph AZA[Availability Zone A]
      APPA[App tier A]
    end
    subgraph AZB[Availability Zone B]
      APPB[App tier B]
      DB[Primary DB]
    end
    NAT[NAT Gateway]
    IGW[Internet Gateway]
  end
  S3[S3 bucket]
  WORLD[Anonymous internet]
  APPA -->|cross-AZ hop ~0.01 per GB| DB
  APPB --> DB
  APPA --> NAT
  APPB --> NAT
  NAT --> IGW
  IGW -->|egress billed per GB| S3
  S3 -.->|public read policy| WORLD`},{id:`failover-drill`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The failover drill nobody ran`,caption:`The 03:12 incident as a sequence: replication was healthy, promotion was not, and a quota wall blocked the scale-out.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant U as Users
  participant LB as Load balancer
  participant A as App tier
  participant P as Primary DB AZ-b
  participant S as Standby DB AZ-a
  U->>LB: checkout request
  LB->>A: route to healthy node
  A->>P: write order
  P-->>S: async replication
  Note over P,S: 03:12 - AZ-b goes dark
  A--xP: write times out
  A->>S: can you take writes?
  S-->>A: no - failover was never automated
  Note over A: 03:41 - on-call improvises a promotion`}],takeaways:[`Meter the physics: cross-AZ transfer is ~$0.01/GB each way — co-locate chatty hot paths in one AZ or pay a distance tax on every call.`,`A NAT gateway idles at ~$33/month plus ~$0.045/GB processed — justify it per subnet and prefer VPC endpoints for S3 and API traffic.`,`Rehearse AZ and region failover at least twice a year with real promotion and real quota checks — an untested runbook fails its first live test.`,`Set billing alarms at 50/80/100% of budget and review service quotas quarterly; both failure modes are silent until they're a Sev-2.`,`Deny by default at the org level: Block Public Access on, IAM roles scoped to the handful of actions they actually call, and zero static service-account keys.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — 15 Cloud Pitfalls`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-cloud-pitfalls.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-frugal-architecture`,pathId:`the-modern-stack`,order:6,title:`Amazon Frugal Architecture`,subtitle:`Werner Vogels' 2024 manifesto: cost belongs on the architecture diagram. Most teams optimise for correctness and performance; cost is what the CFO worries about. Frugal architectur`,minutes:9,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`7 laws`,`Werner Vogels`,`cost-conscious design`],story:{headline:`The board question nobody's architecture could answer`,paragraphs:[`A 40-engineer fintech, Series B, growing fast. The cloud bill is growing three times faster than revenue, but nobody notices because the bill isn't anyone's job. Then board-prep week arrives and the CFO asks one question: "What does a single customer cost us to serve?" Silence. No tags, no attribution, no dashboards. To the finance team the entire cloud spend is one line item called 'AWS'.`,`An emergency audit week follows. The findings are brutal in their boringness: 38% of spend is cross-AZ chatter between 23 microservices that were split for org-chart reasons, 22% is log ingestion nobody has queried since the seed round, 15% is dev clusters idling politely through every night and weekend. Nothing is a bug. Everything is a default.`,`There is no heroic rewrite. The team enforces tags in Terraform, caps log retention at 30 days, schedules non-prod shutdown, moves batch to spot with checkpointing. The bill drops 41% in one quarter. The only application code change is a retry loop. The most expensive thing in the company turned out to be a decision nobody remembered making.`],problem:`Cost was invisible at design time and unattributed at runtime — no one could answer 'what does this feature cost per request?', so no one could optimize it.`},tech:{stack:[`Cost as NFR`,`The 7 Laws`,`IaC Tag Policy`,`Cost Attribution`,`Budget Alerts`,`Spot + Checkpointing`,`Tiered Storage`,`Right-sizing`],note:`Cost enters as a design-time requirement, gets attributed at runtime through tagging and billing exports, and is enforced with alerts, limits, and scheduled controls — closing the loop back into architecture review.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why did cost never make it onto the diagram?`,body:[`Let me start with the uncomfortable question. Cloud was sold as infinite elasticity, and we all heard the implicit promise: capacity is no longer the constraint. True — but the constraint moved. It became the budget. For a decade we optimized latency, throughput, and correctness because those were the things on the whiteboard, and cost was the thing on the CFO's spreadsheet. Two different worlds, and never the twain met — until the bill outgrew the payroll.`,`Here's Vogels' move, and it's genuinely sharp: cost is a non-functional requirement, full stop. It sits on the architecture diagram next to availability and p99. And once it's there, the questions change. An architecture that hits 50ms p99 at $100 per request isn't a fast architecture — it's a failing one. If latency gets an SLO, a target, an owner, and a review, then dollars deserve exactly the same treatment.`,`So the first reframe for your team this week: stop asking 'is it fast?' and start asking 'is it fast at what price?' The CFO is a stakeholder of your architecture whether you invited them or not.`],quote:`If latency gets an SLO, dollars deserve one too.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Typical treatment`,`Frugal question`],rows:[[`Latency`,`p99 SLO, dashboards`,`What does each millisecond cost?`],[`Availability`,`99.9% target, error budgets`,`What does each nine cost?`],[`Throughput`,`Load tests, capacity plans`,`What's the margin per request?`],[`Cost`,`Finance's problem, reviewed quarterly`,`Cost per request, per customer, per feature`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Amazon Frugal Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frugal-architecture.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the seven laws out loud`,body:[`Let me walk the seven laws the way I'd tell them to a team, not the way a slide deck does. Laws one and two are about intent: cost is a non-functional requirement, and systems that last align cost to business. Law two is the one I'd tattoo on the design doc template — if cost-per-customer grows faster than revenue-per-customer, you don't have a successful system, you have a countdown timer. Usage curves should move together or the model is broken.`,`Law three is the honest one: architecting is a series of trade-offs, and cost is always one side of the trade. Cheaper storage means colder reads. Spot means preemption. More cache means staler data. The sin isn't choosing the cheap option — it's pretending the cheap option is free. Laws four and five are the operational meat: unobserved systems lead to unknown costs, and cost-aware architectures implement controls. We'll spend a whole block on those two.`,`And laws six and seven are about time. Optimization is incremental — most savings are many small wins, not one heroic rewrite. And unchallenged success leads to assumptions: 'we've always used Redshift' is not an architecture decision, it's a habit with a budget line. Architectures decay against the moving frontier of what cloud providers offer this year.`],quote:`A system whose costs grow faster than its revenue isn't successful — it's a countdown.`,table:{headers:[`Law`,`Question it forces`,`Where it bites`],rows:[[`1. Cost is an NFR`,`What's the cost per request?`,`Architecture review`],[`2. Align cost to business`,`Does unit cost scale with unit revenue?`,`Business model, pricing`],[`3. Trade-offs`,`What am I paying with for this saving?`,`Every design doc`],[`4. Unobserved = unknown`,`Can I attribute this dollar to a feature?`,`Tagging, billing exports`],[`5. Implement controls`,`What limits this queue, log, or cluster?`,`Runtime guardrails`],[`6. Incremental`,`What 5% can I ship this sprint?`,`Quarterly ops cadence`],[`7. Challenge success`,`Would we buy this again today?`,`Periodic stack audits`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Amazon Frugal Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frugal-architecture.html`}},{heading:`03 · The trade-off ledger: cheap is never free`,body:[`Now the block where I save you from your future self. Every cost optimization on the menu is a trade wearing a discount sticker. Spot instances save roughly 70% on batch workloads — and will yank the rug at the worst moment, so you need checkpointing or idempotent jobs. Tiered storage saves around 80% on data older than 90 days — and doubles the time to pull evidence during an incident investigation. Aggressive caching can cut read-path costs by 90% — and hands you a staleness and invalidation problem that will page you at 3 AM.`,`The discipline isn't avoiding these trades. It's naming them in the design doc before production names them for you. 'We're moving logs to cold storage; forensic queries will take minutes instead of seconds; here's the incident runbook adjustment' — that's an engineering decision. 'We moved logs to cold storage' discovered during a Sev-1 — that's a story for the postmortem.`,`So my rule for design reviews: every cost optimization PR must carry a 'what we pay with' line. If the author can't fill it in, the optimization isn't understood yet, and it ships next quarter, not this one.`],quote:`There is no cheap option — only a trade you haven't named yet.`,table:{headers:[`Optimization`,`You save`,`You pay with`],rows:[[`Spot for batch`,`~70% on those workloads`,`Preemption; needs checkpointing`],[`Tiered storage`,`~80% on data >90 days old`,`Slower cold reads during incidents`],[`Aggressive caching`,`~90% on read-heavy paths`,`Staleness, invalidation complexity`],[`Committed-use discounts`,`~30–60% on baseline`,`Lock-in, forecast risk`],[`Scheduled non-prod shutdown`,`~70% on non-prod`,`Slower weekend work, on-call friction`],[`Async batch over realtime`,`Large on non-urgent paths`,`Added latency, ordering complexity`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Amazon Frugal Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frugal-architecture.html`}},{heading:`04 · Observability for dollars: tag it, attribute it, gate it`,body:[`Laws four and five are where frugal architecture stops being a philosophy and becomes engineering. Law four: unobserved systems lead to unknown costs. If you cannot tag your spend by team, feature, and customer, you cannot optimize anything — you're tuning a system you can't see. Cost attribution is telemetry, same as traces and metrics. Tag resources at creation in your IaC, enforce the policy, export billing data, and dashboard cost per feature next to latency per feature.`,`Law five is the one that pays for itself fastest: controls. Budgets, alerts, kill switches, hard limits. An unbounded queue is also an unbounded bill. An unbounded log stream is an unbounded storage tier with a retention policy of 'forever'. Every autoscaling group without a max is a promise your finance team didn't sign up for. Build the limits in at deploy time, not at invoice time.`,`The feedback loop is the whole game: deploy with tags, attribute spend, alert on anomalies — I like a 20% week-over-week jump on any line item — and route that signal back into architecture review. That's when 'what's the cost per request?' becomes as routine a question as 'what's the p99?'`],quote:`An unbounded queue is an unbounded bill — limits are a cost control, not just a safety one.`,table:{headers:[`Control`,`Catches`,`Cost of the control`],rows:[[`IaC tag policy`,`Unattributed spend`,`Tagging discipline at deploy time`],[`Budget anomaly alerts`,`Runaway growth early`,`Occasional false alarms`],[`Retention and size limits`,`Log and storage creep`,`Shorter forensic window`],[`Autoscaling max caps`,`Runaway scale-out events`,`Capacity headroom planning`],[`Per-feature cost dashboards`,`Unit-cost regressions`,`Dashboard upkeep`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Amazon Frugal Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frugal-architecture.html`}},{heading:`05 · Incremental wins beat heroic rewrites`,body:[`Last block, and it's the one that actually gets funded. Law six: cost optimization is incremental. The fantasy is the 10× rewrite that fixes everything; the reality is that most savings come from a pile of small, boring moves. Right-size instances quarterly — that's roughly 30% on compute. Shut down dev and staging nights and weekends — roughly 70% of your non-prod spend. Tier data older than 90 days, drop unused indexes, cut log retention, compress backups. None of these are glamorous. All of them ship in a sprint.`,`And pair that with law seven: schedule the 'would we buy this today?' audit. Every six months, walk the stack and ask, honestly, which components you'd choose again at current prices and current offerings. The cloud frontier moves under you — last year's sensible default is this year's line item with a cheaper, managed alternative. Unchallenged success is how a 2021 architecture bills you in 2025.`,`So the cadence I recommend: every quarter, rank your top five cost line items, pick two, name the trade, ship the control, measure the unit cost, lock it in with IaC and an alert. Repeat. That loop, run four times a year, compounds into the 40% story from our opening — no rewrite required.`],quote:`Ship the 5% saving this sprint; the 10× rewrite is usually procrastination in a trench coat.`,table:{headers:[`Quarterly move`,`Typical saving`,`Effort`],rows:[[`Right-size instances`,`~30% on compute`,`Low`],[`Kill idle non-prod`,`~70% of non-prod spend`,`Low`],[`Tier storage >90 days`,`~80% on that tier`,`Low–medium`],[`Drop unused indexes`,`~20% on database`,`Low`],[`Committed-use on baseline`,`~30–60% on steady load`,`Medium (commitment)`],[`Compress and dedup backups`,`~30–80% on backup storage`,`Low`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`cost-loop-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Cost-aware architecture: the tag → attribute → control → review loop`,caption:`Cost flows through the same lifecycle as any telemetry: declared at deploy time, attributed at runtime, enforced with controls, and fed back into architecture review.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph Deploy["Deploy-time"]
    IaC["IaC templates"] --> Tag["Tag policy: team, feature, env"]
    Tag --> Res["Provisioned resources"]
  end
  subgraph Observe["Runtime observability"]
    Res --> Billing["Billing export"]
    Billing --> Attr["Spend attribution"]
    Attr --> Dash["Per-feature cost dashboards"]
  end
  subgraph Control["Controls"]
    Dash --> Alert["Anomaly alerts: +20% week over week"]
    Alert --> Gate["Kill switches, retention limits, autoscaling caps"]
    Gate --> Res
  end
  Dash --> Review["Architecture review: cost per request"]
  Review --> IaC`},{id:`incremental-optimization-loop`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The quarterly incremental optimization loop`,caption:`Law six as a runnable process: rank, name the trade, apply the control, measure unit cost, lock in or revert — then repeat next quarter.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  A["Quarterly cost audit"] --> B["Rank top 5 line items"]
  B --> C{"Trade-off named?"}
  C -- "no" --> E["Document the trade in the design doc"]
  E --> D["Apply control: right-size, tier, schedule, cap"]
  C -- "yes" --> D
  D --> F["Measure unit cost: dollars per request"]
  F --> G{"Saving at least 5%?"}
  G -- "yes" --> H["Lock in: IaC change plus budget alert"]
  G -- "no" --> I["Revert or iterate next quarter"]
  H --> A
  I --> A`}],takeaways:[`Put cost-per-request on the same dashboard as p99 latency; give it an explicit SLO (e.g., under $0.001 per request) and review it in every architecture review like any other NFR.`,`Tag 100% of resources at creation via IaC with team, feature, and environment; if you can't attribute a dollar to a feature within one billing cycle, you can't optimize it.`,`Alert when any billing line item grows more than 20% week-over-week, and set hard limits on queues, log retention, and autoscaling maxes — an unbounded queue is an unbounded bill.`,`Bank incremental wins every quarter: right-sizing (~30% on compute), shutting down non-prod nights and weekends (~70% of non-prod), and tiering data older than 90 days (~80% on that tier) — no rewrite required.`,`Run a 'would we buy this today?' stack audit every 6 months; if cost per customer grows faster than revenue per customer, the architecture is a countdown, not a success.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Amazon Frugal Architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-frugal-architecture.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-scale-microservices`,pathId:`the-modern-stack`,order:7,title:`Scaling Microservices`,subtitle:`"Scale microservices" usually means one specific thing: take the synchronous fan-out, replace it with an asynchronous fan-out, and put a capability boundary in front. The fancy nam`,minutes:10,difficulty:4.5,tags:[`BFF`,`aggregator`,`async fan-out`,`capability splitting`],story:{headline:`2:47 AM: twelve green dashboards and a homepage that forgot how to load.`,paragraphs:[`The pager went off at 2:47 AM: homepage p99 at 9.4 seconds, error rate climbing. Every service dashboard was green — except recommendations, freshly deployed, p99 at 4 seconds in a GC-thrash spiral. But the gateway made twelve synchronous calls to render one page, so a 4-second hop meant a 4-second page, and 9 seconds once retries piled on. Twelve green dashboards. One dead homepage.`,`The on-call did what on-call always does first: bumped timeouts, added retries. The retries made it worse — three concurrent attempts per request against a service that was already drowning. Rolling back the deploy recovered recommendations, and the homepage came back. But the postmortem landed on an uncomfortable sentence: our architecture converts any single slow service into a total outage, and no amount of dashboard-green changes that math.`,`Six weeks later the shape was different: three per-client BFFs, event-fed materialized views for the expensive sections, explicit deadlines with per-slice degradation. The next time recommendations melted — and it did — the homepage loaded in 120ms with a 'Suggested for you' skeleton where the slow slice lived. Nobody got paged. That's the whole technique.`],problem:`A synchronous fan-out makes the slowest downstream the page's latency ceiling and turns any single degraded service into a total outage; the fix is per-client BFFs with deadline-bounded fan-out plus event-fed materialized views, so reads stop paying the fan-out tax.`},tech:{stack:[`BFF`,`Async Fan-Out`,`Event Queue`,`Materialized Views`,`CQRS`,`SSE Streaming`],note:`Per-client BFFs own auth, deadline-bounded fan-out, and response shaping; an event queue feeds materialized views so expensive multi-service reads become cache-speed lookups. Page latency becomes the max of a few local reads instead of the sum of twelve remote hops.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the sync fan-out quietly kills you`,body:[`Here's the thing nobody tells you when you split the monolith: the moment your gateway makes twelve synchronous calls to render one page, you've built a distributed single point of latency. Sequential calls? Latency is the sum. Parallel calls? Latency is the max — better, but now any one degraded service holds the entire page hostage. And 'just add timeouts' sounds great until you realize a timeout is a failure you've scheduled on purpose.`,`Why not retries? Because retries against a struggling downstream are an amplifier, not a cure — you just turned 4 seconds of pain into 4 seconds of pain times three concurrent attempts. Why not cache harder at the gateway? Caching helps the warm path, but the cold path and the stale path still fan out, and invalidating caches across twelve schemas is its own full-time job. The problem isn't the services — it's that aggregation happens synchronously, on the critical path, at read time.`,`So the first move is conceptual, not infrastructural: stop paying the fan-out tax on every page view. Either move aggregation off the read path entirely — precompute it — or contain it behind a boundary that can fail slice by slice. That boundary has a name, and we'll get to it.`],quote:`Sum of latencies is the failure mode. Max of latencies is the goal — and a timeout is a failure you scheduled on purpose.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Sync sequential`,`Simple to reason about`,`Latency = sum of all hops`,`Never at scale`],[`Sync parallel`,`Latency = max; easy retrofit`,`One slow or dead hop degrades the whole page`,`Small internal tools`],[`BFF + async fan-out`,`Latency = max of local reads; per-slice degradation`,`New components to own and operate`,`Any client-facing page`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Scaling Microservices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-scale-microservices.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: one BFF per client`,body:[`BFF — Backend for Frontend. One purpose-built API per client surface: web, iOS, Android, partner. The client talks to exactly one thing, and that thing owns five jobs: authenticate the client, decide which internal services matter for this screen, fan out with explicit deadlines, compose the response, cache aggressively where it can. Everything behind it is invisible — the BFF is the client's pseudonym for 'the backend.'`,`Why per-client and not one universal API? Because the iOS app wants different fields, different cardinality, different error shapes than web. A shared API forces every client into the same compromise, and the compromise tax compounds with every new surface you add. A per-client BFF is boring duplication in exchange for independent evolution — and that's a trade you take every single time.`,`The discipline that matters: the BFF is where the fan-out lives, so the BFF is where the deadlines live. Every downstream call gets an explicit timeout, and a missed timeout means that one slice renders degraded — a skeleton, a cached value, a 'can't load right now' — never a failed page.`],quote:`The BFF is the client's pseudonym for the backend — and the only place where 'slow' is allowed to become 'partial.'`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`One shared API`,`One codebase, one deploy`,`Compromises hurt every client; surfaces couple together`,`Homogeneous clients only`],[`Per-client BFF`,`Each surface optimizes shape, caching, and errors independently`,`N codebases, N deploys`,`Web + mobile + partners`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Scaling Microservices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-scale-microservices.html`}},{heading:`03 · Push, don't pull: async aggregation`,body:[`Now the expensive part: the home feed. Instead of 'on every refresh, fan out and assemble the feed,' maintain the feed as a materialized view, updated by events from upstream services. Writes flow through the queue; reads become cache lookups. This is the Twitter home-timeline shape, and Instagram arrived at the same place independently — when two of the biggest feed systems on earth converge on the same pattern, pay attention.`,`It's CQRS at the edge: the write path validates, saves, publishes an event; the read path serves from a denormalized store shaped exactly like the screen. The write store and read store are different shapes on purpose — each scales independently, and the read side stops caring whether recommendations is having a bad day.`,`The honest trade is eventual consistency: the feed might be seconds stale. For a home feed, users don't notice; for a bank balance, they would. So apply this where staleness is cheap and fan-out is expensive — and keep a synchronous fallback for the slices where freshness is genuinely non-negotiable.`],quote:`Precompute at write time what you'd otherwise pay for at read time, a million times a day.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Read latency`,`Freshness`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Pull on read (sync fan-out)`,`100ms–4s, worst case unbounded`,`Always fresh`,`Low-traffic admin screens`],[`Event-fed materialized view`,`Single-digit ms cache lookup`,`Seconds stale`,`Feeds, dashboards, home pages`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Scaling Microservices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-scale-microservices.html`}},{heading:`04 · Stream the seams: degrade slices, not the page`,body:[`Last piece, and it's the one users actually feel: don't wait for the whole payload. Stream the response — SSE, HTTP/2, server components, whatever your stack calls it — and ship the sections that are ready. The header, the nav, and the first card land in under 100 milliseconds; the slow slice arrives whenever it arrives, or shows a skeleton.`,`Perceived performance is a different axis from total latency. An 800ms full payload that shows something at 100ms feels faster than a 400ms payload that shows nothing until 400ms. And because each slice degrades independently, a dead recommendations service becomes a missing box on the page — not a 3 AM page to your on-call.`],quote:`Users don't experience your p99 — they experience the first 100 milliseconds.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Scaling Microservices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-scale-microservices.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`before-sync-fanout`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Before: the synchronous fan-out trap`,caption:`One gateway, twelve synchronous calls — the slowest downstream sets the page's latency ceiling, and any degraded service takes the whole page down with it.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  U["Client"] --> GW["API Gateway<br/>sync fan-out"]
  GW --> S1["Profile · 40ms"]
  GW --> S2["Orders · 90ms"]
  GW --> S3["Inventory · 60ms"]
  GW --> S4["Recommendations · 4000ms"]
  GW --> S5["8 more services"]
  S4 -.->|"one slow hop gates the page"| GW`},{id:`after-bff-async`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`After: BFF + async aggregation`,caption:`Clients hit per-client BFFs that read from event-fed materialized views; the write path flows through a queue, so page latency is a few local reads instead of twelve remote hops.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph CL["Client surfaces"]
    W["Web"]
    I["iOS"]
    A["Android"]
  end
  W --> WB["Web BFF"]
  I --> IB["iOS BFF"]
  A --> AB["Android BFF"]
  WB --> RS[("Materialized<br/>read store")]
  IB --> RS
  AB --> RS
  subgraph DM["Domain services · write path"]
    P["Profile"]
    O["Orders"]
    R["Recommendations"]
  end
  P --> Q[["Event queue"]]
  O --> Q
  R --> Q
  Q --> M["View materializer"]
  M --> RS`}],takeaways:[`Latency math: a sync fan-out page costs the sum (sequential) or max (parallel) of downstream p99s — keep every critical-path downstream under 100ms or evict it from the critical path.`,`Timeout rule: set every BFF→service deadline at roughly 2× that call's p50 (cap page-critical hops at ~250ms) and render the missed slice degraded — never fail the whole page.`,`One BFF per client surface; once a shared API serves 3+ clients with divergent shapes, the compromise tax exceeds the duplication cost — split it.`,`Materialize any read that costs 3+ downstream calls or >50ms of assembly into an event-updated view; reads drop to single-digit-ms cache lookups at the price of seconds of staleness.`,`Stream the response: get real content on screen in under 100ms via SSE/HTTP2 and let slow slices trail in — perceived latency drops even when total latency doesn't.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Scaling Microservices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-scale-microservices.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`yt-DXZynfHZJYk`,pathId:`the-modern-stack`,order:8,kind:`video`,title:`Docker Explained in 10 Minutes: Dockerfiles, Layer Caches, Virtualization`,subtitle:`Transcript not retrieved yet. The video is here; the written reasoning is still being prepared.`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`the-modern-stack`],story:{headline:`The 3 AM hotfix stuck behind a 38-minute build`,paragraphs:["It's 2:47 AM. PagerDuty fires: checkout error rate at 30%. The fix is three lines — a bad regex in the tax calculator. I push it, open CI, and the build timer says `estimated: 38 minutes`. Behind it, a queue of eleven other builds. The incident bridge goes quiet in that specific way it does when six engineers are collectively watching a progress bar.","The Dockerfile looked innocent. But line 3 was `COPY . .` — placed *before* `npm install`. Source changes on every commit, so every commit invalidated that layer and everything beneath it, including the 1,400-package dependency install. And when the image finally did build, it weighed 1.9 GB: a full Node toolchain image with compilers, git, and three package managers production never executes, shipped to every node on every deploy.",`We reordered four lines, split the build into two stages, and swapped the base to a slim image. Build time went from 38 minutes to 90 seconds. Image size went from 1.9 GB to 190 MB. Nobody had to learn anything new — we just stopped fighting how Docker actually works.`],problem:`A Dockerfile ordered for human readability instead of cache locality turns every commit into a full rebuild and every deploy into a gigabyte-scale transfer.`},tech:{stack:[`Docker`,`Dockerfile`,`Layer Cache`,`OverlayFS`,`Namespaces`,`cgroups`,`Multi-stage Builds`,`BuildKit`],note:`Docker runs an app as a process isolated by kernel namespaces and capped by cgroups, with the image assembled from immutable overlayfs layers. The Dockerfile is the build recipe, and its instruction order determines exactly what the layer cache can reuse.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: a container is just a process in a costume`,body:[`Let's kill the biggest misconception first: a container is not a tiny virtual machine. A VM runs a hypervisor that emulates hardware, then boots an entire guest OS inside it — that's why VM images weigh gigabytes and take minutes to come up. A container is a regular Linux process that the kernel has agreed to lie to: namespaces give it a private view of PIDs, network interfaces, mounts, and hostname, while cgroups put hard ceilings on its CPU and memory.`,`Because there's no second kernel in play, startup is milliseconds and you can pack dozens of containers per host where you'd fit a handful of VMs. So why not go even lighter — just chroot? Because chroot only fakes the filesystem: no network isolation, no process isolation, no resource limits, and one runaway process takes down the whole box. And why not VMs everywhere? You get stronger isolation and cross-OS support, but you pay for it in boot time, memory overhead, and density. For application workloads, containers sit squarely in the sweet spot.`],quote:`A container isn't a tiny VM — it's a normal process that's been told exactly what it's allowed to see.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`VM`,`Strong isolation, any guest OS`,`GB-scale images, minutes to boot`,`Different kernels, hard multi-tenancy`],[`Container`,`ms startup, high density, shared kernel`,`Weaker isolation, same-kernel only`,`Apps, CI, microservices`],[`chroot / bare process`,`Zero overhead`,`No net/pid isolation, no resource caps`,`Trusted single-tenant scripts`]]}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: every line is a cache key`,body:[`Here's the mental shift that fixes 90% of slow builds: every instruction in a Dockerfile produces a layer, and the builder checks each one against its cache — same instruction text, same input checksums (for COPY/ADD that's content hashes of the files), same parent layer — and if it all matches, the cached layer is reused for free.`,"The catch is the cascade: if layer N misses, every layer after it rebuilds, even if their inputs didn't change. That's why `COPY . .` on line 3 was fatal — source changes every commit, so the dependency install below it re-ran on every commit. The fix is ordering by volatility: base image first (changes ~never), dependency manifests and install second (changes on bumps), source code third (changes every commit), build args and CI metadata last.",`And the reverse question — why not just COPY everything up front and keep the Dockerfile 'simple'? Because 'simple' costs you a full dependency reinstall on every single commit. The Dockerfile isn't a recipe; it's a chain of cache keys, and the order is the entire game.`],quote:`The Dockerfile isn't a recipe — it's a chain of cache keys, and order is the entire game.`,table:{headers:[`Layer (top to bottom)`,`Invalidated when`,`Typical rebuild cost`],rows:[[`FROM pinned base image`,`Base digest changes (rare)`,`Minutes, once`],[`COPY lockfile + RUN npm ci`,`Lockfile changes`,`~1-2 min`],[`COPY src/ + RUN build`,`Every commit`,`Seconds`],[`ARG / LABEL build metadata`,`Every build`,`Free — keep it last`]]}},{heading:`03 · Second thought: why not just cache everything?`,body:["Now the pushback I get on every webinar: 'if caching is so great, why not cache everything forever?' Because layers are immutable, inspectable, and effectively forever. Run `apt-get update` in a layer and that package index is frozen at build time — six months later you're installing from a stale, possibly vulnerable index. Run a curl with a token in a RUN and the token lives in layer history; anyone who can pull the image can read it with `docker history`.","The modern fixes are surgical: BuildKit cache mounts (`--mount=type=cache`) keep the package manager's cache warm across builds without baking it into a layer, and secret mounts feed credentials to a RUN without ever writing them into the image. And `.dockerignore` is the cheapest win in all of Docker — without it, `COPY . .` drags in `.git` (hundreds of MB, and it busts the cache on every commit), `node_modules`, and any `.env` file lying around the repo.",`One more boundary while we're here: the writable top layer. Containers write there copy-on-write, which is perfect for ephemeral scratch data and terrible for anything you want to keep — a database writing to its container layer loses everything on restart and pays a copy-on-write tax on every write. That's exactly what volumes exist for.`],quote:`Layers are immutable and public — if it happened in a RUN, assume the whole internet can read it.`,table:{headers:[`Hazard`,`Symptom`,`Fix`],rows:[[`Cached apt/apk index`,`Stale, vulnerable packages`,`Cache mounts or deliberate cache-bust`],[`Secrets in RUN layers`,`Credentials readable via docker history`,`BuildKit --mount=type=secret`],[`No .dockerignore`,`.git + node_modules in context, cache busts`,`Ignore list before the first build`],[`State written to container layer`,`Data lost on restart, slow writes`,`Named volumes for anything persistent`]]}},{heading:`04 · The size problem: who put a compiler in production?`,body:[`Second half of the 3 AM story: even when the build finished, we were shipping 1.9 GB. Do the math — at 50 MB/s that's roughly 40 seconds per node per pull; across a 50-node fleet that's half an hour of aggregate registry traffic on every deploy, and far worse when nodes have cold caches. The weight was compilers, dev dependencies, git, and build tooling that production never executes.`,`Multi-stage builds fix this structurally: stage one is a 'build' stage with the full toolchain that compiles your artifact; stage two starts FROM a clean runtime base and COPYs only the artifact. The compilers never enter the final image — they don't just get skipped, they literally don't exist in it.`,`Base image choice is the remaining dial. Alpine is ~5 MB but uses musl libc, which occasionally bites native modules compiled against glibc. Debian slim (~75 MB) is the boring, safe default. Distroless goes further — no shell, no package manager — which is fantastic for attack surface and painful for debugging. Match the base to how much you value size versus debuggability.`],quote:`Your production image should contain your app and its runtime — nothing that built it.`,table:{headers:[`Base image`,`Size`,`Trade-off`,`Fit`],rows:[[`alpine`,`~5 MB`,`musl libc quirks with native deps`,`Static binaries, size-critical`],[`debian-slim`,`~75 MB`,`glibc, slightly larger`,`Default for most services`],[`distroless`,`~2-20 MB`,`No shell — hard to debug`,`Security-sensitive production`],[`Full toolchain image`,`~1-2 GB`,`Everything shipped to prod`,`Build stage only`]]}},{heading:`05 · Putting it together: the 90-second pipeline`,body:["So here's the final shape of the Dockerfile that took us from 38 minutes to 90 seconds: a slim base pinned by digest, lockfile copy plus dependency install with a cache mount, source copy, the build in stage one, a runtime stage two running as a non-root USER, and a `.dockerignore` written before the first build ever ran.","In CI we point BuildKit at a registry cache (`--cache-to` / `--cache-from type=registry`), so even a runner with a cold local cache pulls only the layers that changed — a few megabytes, not gigabytes. And when something looks slow, we audit instead of guessing: `docker history` shows what each layer costs, and tools like dive make it visual. Fast builds aren't a Docker feature you switch on — they're a Dockerfile you order correctly."],quote:`Fast builds aren't a Docker feature you enable — they're a Dockerfile you order correctly.`}],diagrams:[{id:`image-anatomy`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of an image: stacked layers over a shared kernel`,caption:`Read-only layers stack via overlayfs; the container adds one copy-on-write writable layer and runs as a namespaced process on the host kernel — no guest OS anywhere.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph IMG["Docker image — read-only layers"]
        direction TB
        L1["Layer 1 · debian:bookworm-slim · ~75 MB"]
        L2["Layer 2 · lockfile copy + npm ci · ~180 MB"]
        L3["Layer 3 · COPY src/ · ~2 MB"]
        L1 --> L2 --> L3
    end
    subgraph CTR["Container at runtime"]
        direction TB
        W["Writable top layer · copy-on-write"]
        P["App process · PID + net + mount namespaces · cgroup caps"]
        W --> P
    end
    L3 --> W
    K["Host Linux kernel · shared by all containers"]
    P -->|"syscalls"| K
    VM["VM comparison · guest OS + hypervisor · ~1-2 GB · minutes to boot"]`},{id:`cache-hit-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`A 90-second CI build: cache hits until the source layer`,caption:`BuildKit checks each layer top-down; only the changed source layer and its downstream layers rebuild, and only those get pushed to the registry.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant CI as CI runner
    participant BK as BuildKit
    participant LC as Layer cache
    participant REG as Registry
    CI->>BK: docker build .
    BK->>LC: FROM node:20-bookworm-slim
    LC-->>BK: hit
    BK->>LC: COPY package-lock.json
    LC-->>BK: hit
    BK->>LC: RUN npm ci
    LC-->>BK: hit — 0 s, zero network
    BK->>LC: COPY src/
    LC-->>BK: miss — new commit
    BK->>BK: rebuild downstream layers (npm run build)
    BK->>REG: push changed layers (~3 MB)
    BK-->>CI: image ready in ~90 s`}],takeaways:[`Order Dockerfile instructions least-to-most volatile — manifests and dependency install before source COPY; reordering four lines took our build from 38 min to 90 s.`,`One changed layer invalidates every layer after it — treat each instruction as a cache key and keep per-build metadata (ARG, LABEL) at the very end.`,`Ship multi-stage runtime images under ~200 MB on slim/distroless bases; a 1.9 GB image costs ~40 s per node per pull at 50 MB/s — half an hour of aggregate fleet traffic per deploy.`,`Containers share the host kernel via namespaces + cgroups: millisecond startup and VM-beating density, but no cross-OS isolation — use VMs when you need a different kernel.`,`Add .dockerignore and BuildKit secret mounts before your first build: never let .git, node_modules, or credentials enter the context or a layer — docker history exposes every RUN forever.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Hello Interview — Docker Explained in 10 Minutes: Dockerfiles, Layer Caches, Virtualization`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DXZynfHZJYk`,author:`Hello Interview`,note:`Transcript not retrieved yet. The video is here; the written reasoning is still being prepared.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`pending`}];export{e as lessons};